  <p class="blog-lede" slot="lede">
    After a quiet stretch on the blog desk, we wanted a single reference piece that answers the question shoppers actually type into search: <strong>what brands show up on this site?</strong>
    The short answer is that <strong><a href="/">kakobuy-cn.com</a></strong> indexes well over <strong>140 distinct brand labels</strong> across roughly <strong>1,000+ on-site catalogue rows</strong> (as of our June&nbsp;2026 snapshot), organised into the same twelve lanes you see on the homepage—shoes, jackets, hoodies, bags, fragrance, electronics, and the rest.
    The longer answer requires honesty up front: we are <em>not</em> Louis Vuitton, Nike, or Kakobuy’s checkout team. We publish a moderated <strong><a href="/">Kakobuy Spreadsheet</a></strong> directory and editorial guides; commerce still happens only after you paste marketplace URLs into <a href="https://kakobuy.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kakobuy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Read this before you treat any row as “official”</h2>
  <p>
    Brand names on spreadsheet-style hubs describe <strong>what a listing is trying to evoke</strong>—silhouette, logo placement, materials language—not a certificate of authorised distribution.
    Taobao, Weidian, and 1688 sellers use those names in titles, albums, and batch chatter; our taxonomy mirrors that reality so you can filter faster.
    kakobuy-cn.com does not manufacture goods, hold inventory, or speak for any fashion house.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Editorial only:</strong> we explain lanes, link hygiene, and QC discipline—see <a href="/how-to-buy/">How to buy</a> for paste workflow.</li>
    <li><strong>Live browse lives elsewhere:</strong> the high-volume grid many veterans use sits on the external <a href="/spreadsheet/">Spreadsheet tutorial hub</a> and Maison Looks–class catalogue bridges we reference throughout the site.</li>
    <li><strong>Your proof is warehouse QC:</strong> seller stills and hub thumbnails are orientation; Kakobuy inbound photos decide whether you approve export—our <a href="/blog/kakobuy-spreadsheet-qc/">QC guide</a> stays the operational bible.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    With that firewall in place, the brand map below is still useful: it tells you <em>which aesthetics this community actually shops</em>, not which press release we signed.
  </p>

  <h2>How we counted brands (methodology)</h2>
  <p>
    Figures in this article come from our <strong>on-site English catalogue export</strong>—the same JSON feed that powers category tiles and product cards—snapshot on <strong>3 June 2026</strong>.
    We deduplicated brand strings exactly as they appear in listing metadata (so “Carhartt” and “Carhartt WIP” stay separate labels, matching how buyers search).
    Counts drift weekly as curators add rows, sellers rotate albums, and dead links get pruned; treat numbers as <em>order-of-magnitude truth</em>, not an audited financial inventory.
  </p>
