  <h2>The six “continents” of our brand map</h2>
  <p>
    Rather than an A–Z phone book, think in <strong>shopping continents</strong>—the mental buckets haul builders use when they open a <strong><a href="/">Kakobuy Spreadsheet</a></strong> tab at midnight.
  </p>

  <h3>1. European luxury &amp; contemporary houses</h3>
  <p>
    This is the densest luxury cluster in our feed. <strong>Louis Vuitton</strong> appears across hoodies, scarves, sandals, jewellery, and bags—often the most repeated name in knitwear lanes.
    <strong>Dior</strong>, <strong>Gucci</strong>, <strong>Fendi</strong>, <strong>Burberry</strong>, <strong>Celine</strong>, <strong>Loewe</strong>, <strong>Givenchy</strong>, <strong>Balenciaga</strong>, <strong>Saint Laurent</strong>, <strong>Prada</strong>, <strong>Miu Miu</strong>, and <strong>Valentino</strong> show up repeatedly in outerwear, eyewear, heels, and leather-goods rows.
    Accessory-heavy labels—<strong>Goyard</strong>, <strong>Coach</strong>, <strong>Chrome Hearts</strong>, <strong>Cartier</strong>, <strong>Tiffany &amp; Co.</strong>, <strong>Hermès</strong> (especially loafers/slides)—anchor the “logo-forward” accessory lanes shoppers screenshot for QC comparisons.
  </p>
  <p>
    Browse starting points: <a href="/category/bags/">Bags</a>, <a href="/category/jackets/">Jackets</a>, <a href="/category/accessories/">Accessories</a>.
    Shipping branded leather and eyewear internationally is friction-heavy—read <a href="/blog/shipping-branded-vs-unbranded-spreadsheet-restrictions/">branded vs. unbranded routing</a> before you promise friends a landed date.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Streetwear, hype, and logo culture</h3>
  <p>
    Streetwear is not a single brand; it is a velocity layer. Our catalogue leans into labels Reddit and Discord already tag constantly:
    <strong>Supreme</strong>, <strong>Stussy</strong>, <strong>Off-White</strong>, <strong>Palace</strong>-adjacent aesthetics via <strong>Corteiz</strong>, <strong>Trapstar</strong>, <strong>Syna World</strong>, <strong>Broken Planet</strong>, <strong>Hellstar</strong>, <strong>SP5DER</strong>, <strong>Represent</strong>, <strong>Rhude</strong>, <strong>Gallery Dept.</strong>, <strong>Vetements</strong>, and <strong>VLONE</strong>-era graphics.
    <strong>A Bathing Ape</strong> is one of the most indexed tee labels on-site (six rows in the t-shirt lane alone in our snapshot).
    <strong>Chrome Hearts</strong> crosses from jewellery into tees, shorts, and phone accessories—useful if you are building a cohesive “silver &amp; black” cart.
  </p>
  <p>
    Browse: <a href="/category/t-shirts/">T-Shirts</a>, <a href="/category/hoodies/">Hoodies/Sweaters</a>, <a href="/category/headwear/">Headwear</a>.
    Sizing chaos is real—pair picks with <a href="/blog/kakobuy-sizing-spreadsheet-apparel/">Spreadsheet sizing notes</a> before you lock variants.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Sportswear, sneakers, and court culture</h3>
  <p>
    Athletic trademarks dominate footwear and jersey lanes. <strong>Nike</strong> and <strong>Jordan</strong> lead sneaker rows (with <strong>New Balance</strong>, <strong>Adidas</strong>, <strong>Yeezy</strong>, <strong>Onitsuka Tiger</strong>, <strong>Golden Goose</strong>, and <strong>Vans</strong> appearing less often but still present).
    Teamwear shows up through <strong>Mitchell &amp; Ness</strong>, <strong>Errea</strong>, <strong>Joma</strong>, and club-adjacent graphics; <strong>Adidas</strong> tracksuits and <strong>Nike</strong> tech fleece intersect with the tracksuit lane.
    Batch culture matters here more than any other continent—identical thumbnails can hide ¥200 vs ¥400 factory tiers. Our <a href="/blog/decoding-spreadsheet-batch-prices-discrepancies/">batch discrepancy primer</a> is essential reading before you chase “LJR” or “GX” nicknames in cells.
  </p>
  <p>
    Browse: <a href="/category/shoes/">Shoes</a>, <a href="/category/jersey/">Jersey</a>, <a href="/category/pants/">Pants/Shorts</a> (for tracksuit bottoms).
  </p>

  <h3>4. Outdoor, workwear, and “gorpcore” technical</h3>
  <p>
    Practical layers appear throughout parka and trousers lanes: <strong>The North Face</strong>, <strong>Arc'teryx</strong>, <strong>Canada Goose</strong>, <strong>Moncler</strong>, <strong>Moose Knuckles</strong>, <strong>Stone Island</strong>, <strong>CP Company</strong>, <strong>Carhartt</strong> / <strong>Carhartt WIP</strong>, <strong>Descente</strong>, and <strong>Patagonia</strong>-adjacent styling via Arc'teryx-heavy rows.
    <strong>Timberland</strong>, <strong>Dr. Martens</strong>, and <strong>Birkenstock</strong> anchor boot lanes; <strong>UGG</strong> and <strong>Crocs</strong> dominate casual sandal/slide clusters.
    These SKUs are volumetric freight traps—puffers and shell jackets explode DIM weight. See <a href="/blog/spreadsheet-volumetric-chargeable-weight-basics/">chargeable weight basics</a> and consider vacuum packaging when Kakobuy offers it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Browse: <a href="/category/jackets/">Jackets</a>, <a href="/category/shoes/">Shoes</a> (boots), <a href="/category/pants/">Pants</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Denim, premium basics, and quiet luxury casuals</h3>
  <p>
    Not every row screams logo. We still index <strong>Amiri</strong>, <strong>Ami Paris</strong>, <strong>Acne Studios</strong>, <strong>Essentials / Fear of God</strong>, <strong>Calvin Klein</strong>, <strong>Tommy Hilfiger</strong>, <strong>Polo Ralph Lauren</strong>, <strong>Lacoste</strong>, <strong>Levi's</strong>, <strong>Diesel</strong>, <strong>Balmain</strong>, and <strong>Karl Lagerfeld</strong> in denim, underwear, and shirt lanes.
    <strong>Amiri</strong> and <strong>Gallery Dept.</strong> repeat in distressed-denim conversations; <strong>Maison Margiela</strong> shows up in deconstructed knitwear rows.
    These pieces are where beginners under-spend on QC and over-spend on returns—measurement charts matter more than logo alignment.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Beauty, fragrance, electronics, and lifestyle hardware</h3>
  <p>
    Fragrance rows read like a department-store counter: <strong>Dior</strong>, <strong>Chanel</strong>, <strong>Gucci</strong>, <strong>Tom Ford</strong>, <strong>Creed</strong>, <strong>Amouage</strong>, <strong>Maison Francis Kurkdjian</strong>, <strong>Jean Paul Gaultier</strong>, <strong>Bvlgari</strong>, <strong>Victoria's Secret</strong>, and designer-adjacent “inspired” bottles.
    Electronics and audio skew pragmatic: <strong>Apple</strong>, <strong>Samsung</strong>, <strong>Sony</strong>, <strong>Beats</strong>, <strong>JBL</strong>, plus lifestyle hardware like <strong>Dyson</strong> in personal-care device lanes.
    Liquid and battery SKUs trigger route restrictions—never assume your spreadsheet row’s brand name alone makes a line customs-safe.
  </p>
  <p>
    Browse: <a href="/category/perfume/">Perfume</a>, <a href="/category/electronics/">Electronics</a>, <a href="/category/other/">Other stuff</a> (underwear, oddments).
  </p>

